.env

# ── Build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/mikrodash
/mikrodashd
*.exe
bin/
# Build output, EXCEPT two frozen recordings that happen to live inside it.
#
# `live-dhcp.js` and `live-routers.js` are lifted from the Node renderer by
# tools/live-renderer.js, and dhcp-table-check and routers-grid-check compare the
# port against them. They are recordings in the same sense as
# testdata/golden-gates/ -- the reference that produced them is gone, so they can
# no longer be regenerated and must travel with the repo.
#
# Git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, which is why
# this is three rules instead of one negation.
web/dist/*
!web/dist/_compare/
web/dist/_compare/*
!web/dist/_compare/live-*.js
web/node_modules/

# Working notes for the current iteration. Cleared after every push.
Changes.md

# ── Agent tooling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ignored WHOLESALE, which is this repo's long-standing rule and deliberately
# unlike the port repo's narrower one. The port committed .claude/ and .serena/
# because they were project knowledge travelling with an unpublished repo; this
# repo is public, so the safe default is that nothing agent-specific is
# published unless somebody decides it should be, file by file.
#
# The practical trigger: the port's .claude/settings.json carried 61 deny rules
# naming this repository as a forbidden EXTERNAL path. Inside this repository
# they would have blocked the agent from editing anything at all.
.claude/
.serena/
docs/superpowers/

# The port channel, closed at cutover (v0.8.0). Both files were untracked AND
# unignored for months, so a single `git add -A` would have published them.
ToDo.md
port_drift_notes.md

# ── Browser tooling scratch ───────────────────────────────────────────────
.playwright-mcp/

# Screenshots taken to check a change looks right. Worthless a day later, so
# never committed. The Playwright MCP server also writes one CONSOLE LOG per
# session here, and those are not scratch: measured on the port repo, 6.0 MB had
# accumulated carrying WAN and LAN addresses, device hostnames and VPN provider
# names — everything a page rendered while a session drove the browser.
#
# Real assets live in screenshots/ and web/public/, which are tracked.
.screenshots/

# Belt and braces for the same thing, ROOT-ANCHORED on purpose. 178 stray
# screenshots accumulated in the repo root before .screenshots/ existed. The
# port repo used a bare `*.png`, which would also ignore the eleven tracked
# images under screenshots/ that the README renders.
/*.png

# ── Transient gate bundles ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each gate esbuilds its port half to `testdata/.<name>-port.cjs` and removes it
# when it finishes. An interrupted run leaves one behind, and a stray build
# artefact is not a change anybody wants to review.
testdata/.*-port.cjs
testdata/.*-entry.ts
testdata/.accept-meta.json
# Three gates name their bundle without the `-port` suffix.
testdata/.rs-runs.cjs
testdata/.rt.cjs
testdata/.seam.cjs

# Build-time Go tools, if one is compiled into the tree by mistake.
/tsgen
/webbuild
/geogen

# Bundled web tests — build output, not fixtures.
web/test-out/
